  "query_preview": "(Tonight through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 1120 PM EST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nA relatively uncomplicated forecast remains in the offing for the \nremainder of the week, with largely dry and quiet conditions, well \nabove normal temperatures through the first part of the coming \nweekend, and only a brief opportunity for showers primarily late \nSaturday night into Sunday evening - particularly pleasant \nconditions as we approach the doorstep of the winter season.\n\nAn expansive and strong dome of surface high pressure will remain in \ncontrol across the eastern third of the CONUS through the end of the \nwork week, with upper level ridging also shifting eastward into the \narea and displacing the primary track for disturbances well to our \nnorth.\n\nSkies will be clear to partly cloudy throughout the week, with a \nslow but steady warming trend of about 2-4 degrees each day through \nFriday, bringing highs back to well above normal for the time of \nyear, and lows above normal but still crisp at times with nearly \nideal radiational cooling conditions expected several nights. Some \npatchy fog concern may need watching on several nights, particularly \nin stream valleys and other climatologically favored areas, but with \nample sun on many days, along with over 90 percent of the state at \nleast abnormally dry and moderate drought ongoing across a third of \nIndiana, widespread fog concerns should be minimal.\n\nChange comes again over the weekend as the upper level ridge finally \nbreaks down and the surface ridge is shuttled eastward, allowing an \nelongated shortwave to pass through the region. Associated with this \nshortwave looks to be a moderate surface low north of the Great \nLakes with a lengthy cold front but a relatively narrow warm \nsector/moist conveyor belt as the surface ridge's influence back \ninto the Gulf Coast inhibits early moisture return late in the week. \nThe displacement of the more significant forcing northward along \nwith the relatively narrow moist conveyor belt should limit precip \ncoverage and amounts to at least a slight degree, with chances maximized\nimmediately along and ahead of the front, as this will be the primary\nforcing source in the immediate region, with only weak upright\ninstability expected if any. That said, despite the limited Gulf \nmoisture return, remnant Pacific moisture from the west coast will\nbe drawn into the system as well, helping to mitigate the \naforementioned limitations to some degree, and column precipitable\nwater values may at least briefly approach or exceed \nclimatological 90th percentile, though rain duration and dry \nantecedent conditions will likely keep this from presenting any \nproblems. Frontal timing has slowed slightly and is now more \ndiurnally favorable, but old school K-index values, which have \nutility as a first glance for both surface based and elevated \nconvection, remain unimpressive, and forecast profiles agree with \nlittle to no upright instability depicted.\n\nA return to dry but substantially cooler weather is expected for \nearly next week in the wake of the cold front as another large \nsurface high traverses the Great Lakes region, though this one \nappears to move through a bit more quickly than the one anchored \nover the eastern US this week. Temperatures are likely to be 20 or \nso degrees colder by next Monday than they were to start the weekend.",
